
const missions = {
    eagle: {
        name: 'Apollo 11 — Eagle', dest: 'moon', place: 'Moon', type: 'Apollo 11 Mission · 1969', summary: 'The descent stage left on the Moon', scenes: [
            ['A Historic Mission', 'In July 1969, a great adventure began. The Apollo 11 mission left Earth carrying three astronauts toward the Moon.\n\nTo get there, they needed a very special piece of equipment: the Eagle lunar module.\n\nThe Eagle had two parts. The upper part would carry the astronauts back into lunar orbit. The lower part, called the descent stage, had a different mission: to bring the module safely down to the Moon’s surface.\n\nIt was a journey that would go down in history!', 'Did you know the descent stage had its own rocket engine? It helped slow the lunar module down so it could land safely.'],
            ['Arrival on the Moon', 'On July 20, 1969, the Eagle began its descent toward the Sea of Tranquility.\n\nThe descent stage fired its engine and helped control the module’s movement until it reached the lunar surface.\n\nAfter a careful and closely monitored descent, the Eagle landed!\n\nNeil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin were finally on the Moon. The descent stage had completed one of the mission’s most important tasks: bringing the astronauts down to the lunar surface.', 'Did you know the landing site was the Sea of Tranquility? Despite its name, there is no water there. It is a vast plain formed by ancient volcanic lava.'],
            ['What Was Left Behind?', 'After approximately 21 hours and 36 minutes on the lunar surface, it was time to leave.\n\nThe astronauts used the Eagle’s upper section, called the ascent stage, to return to orbit and rejoin Michael Collins aboard the Columbia spacecraft.\n\nBut the descent stage could not go with them.\n\nIt remained on the Moon, supported by its four legs, at the very site of the first human landing.\n\nA commemorative plaque was also attached to its structure, marking the importance of that achievement.', 'Did you know the plaque left on the descent stage carried a message of peace? It said that human beings from Earth had set foot on the Moon for the first time in July 1969.'],
            ['Why Was It Important?', 'The descent stage was more than just one part of a spacecraft. It was essential in bringing the first astronauts to the lunar surface.\n\nIts engine helped the module land, its structure supported the module, and its platform served as the launch base for the ascent stage.\n\nEven after the astronauts left, it remained as a tangible reminder of that extraordinary mission.\n\nIts story shows that every part of a mission can play a vital role—and that what remains behind can tell a great story too!', 'Did you know the descent stage also served as a launch platform? The ascent stage used it as a base to lift off from the lunar surface and carry the astronauts back into orbit.']],
        quiz: [['What was the Eagle descent stage’s main purpose?', ['To bring the module safely down to the lunar surface', 'Transport Michael Collins to Mars', 'Explore the surface of Mars'], 'To bring the module safely down to the lunar surface'], ['What happened to the descent stage after the astronauts left?', ['It was brought back to Earth', 'It remained on the Moon’s surface', 'It was launched toward the Sun'], 'It remained on the Moon’s surface']]
    },
     surveyor: {
        name: 'Surveyor 3', dest: 'moon', place: 'Moon', type: 'Lunar probe · 1967', summary: 'The machine that was found again', scenes: [
            ['A Historic Mission', 'On April 17, 1967, a machine called Surveyor 3 left Earth on a very important mission: to reach the Moon and discover what the surface of that distant world was like.\n\nIt was equipped with a television camera and a special instrument capable of touching, digging into, and moving the lunar soil.\n\nScientists wanted to find out whether the Moon’s surface was firm enough to support a spacecraft carrying astronauts.\n\nThe little explorer was about to help humanity learn more about the Moon!', 'Did you know Surveyor 3 weighed approximately 998 kg? It had three support legs and was built to make a soft landing on the lunar surface!'],
            ['Arrival on the Moon', 'On April 20, 1967, Surveyor 3 reached the Moon and landed in a region called Oceanus Procellarum.\n\nBut its arrival did not go exactly as planned!\n\nDuring descent, some very bright rocks confused the radar that helped guide the landing. As a result, the spacecraft touched the ground, bounced twice, and finally came to a safe stop.\n\nAfter landing, it began sending images of the lunar landscape back to Earth.', 'Did you know Surveyor 3 bounced twice before landing for good? On its first bounce, it rose approximately 10 meters!'],
            ['What Did It Discover There?', 'After reaching the Moon, Surveyor 3 began investigating the terrain.\n\nIts camera sent 6,326 images to scientists on Earth. In addition, its sampling instrument was able to dig small trenches and perform soil tests.\n\nIt dug four trenches, conducted four bearing tests, and performed thirteen impact tests.\n\nThese experiments helped scientists understand the texture and strength of lunar soil.\n\nThe findings showed that the surface could support the weight of an Apollo lunar module.', 'Did you know Surveyor 3’s sampling arm could reach up to 1.5 meters and dig approximately 18 centimeters deep?'],
            ['Why Was It Important?', 'On May 4, 1967, Surveyor 3 sent its final communication to Earth, two days after lunar night began.\n\nBut its story still had an unexpected chapter.\n\nIn November 1969, more than two years later, astronauts Charles Conrad and Alan Bean arrived on the Moon on the Apollo 12 mission and landed approximately 180 meters from the old explorer.\n\nDuring a moonwalk, they walked over to Surveyor 3, examined its structure, and collected several parts, including its camera and soil-sampling instrument.\n\nThese materials were brought back to Earth so scientists could study the effects of time and exposure to the lunar environment.\n\nSurveyor 3 helped pave the way for the Apollo missions and showed that even a machine left on the Moon could continue contributing to science!', 'Did you know the Apollo 12 astronauts visited Surveyor 3? They walked over to it and brought parts back to Earth to study how they had endured almost two and a half years on the Moon!']],
        quiz: [['What was one of Surveyor 3’s objectives?', ['To study whether the lunar soil could support a spacecraft carrying astronauts', 'Take people to Mars', 'To photograph Saturn’s rings'], 'To study whether the lunar soil could support a spacecraft carrying astronauts'], ['Which mission visited Surveyor 3 in November 1969?', ['Apollo 11', 'Apollo 12', 'Mars 2020'], 'Apollo 12']]
    },
    opportunity: {
        name: 'Opportunity', dest: 'mars', place: 'Mars', type: 'Martian rover · 2004–2018', summary: 'The explorer that uncovered secrets of the Red Planet', scenes: [
            ['A Historic Mission', 'On July 7, 2003, a mission left Earth carrying a small robotic explorer named Opportunity.\n\nIt was one of a pair of twin robots sent by NASA to investigate Mars. Its twin was Spirit, which would explore another region of the planet.\n\nOpportunity had a very important mission: to study Martian rocks and soil in search of clues that liquid water may once have existed on the planet’s surface.\n\nIts adventure was just beginning!', 'Did you know Opportunity was designed to operate for only 90 Martian days? Even so, its mission lasted almost 15 Earth years!'],
            ['Arrival on Mars', 'On January 24, 2004, Opportunity reached the surface of Mars.\n\nIts destination was a region called Meridiani Planum, where scientists had identified minerals that could indicate the past presence of water.\n\nDuring landing, the rover was protected by airbags that helped cushion the impact. It bounced several times across the surface before finally coming to rest inside a small crater called Eagle.\n\nNow it was time to start exploring!', 'Did you know Opportunity bounced at least 26 times during landing? The airbags worked like giant cushions to protect the explorer!'],
            ['What Did It Discover There?', 'During its explorations, Opportunity found evidence that liquid water had been present on Mars long ago.\n\nBy studying rocks and tiny mineral grains, it identified important clues about the planet’s wet past.\n\nAmong its discoveries were small, hematite-rich mineral spheres nicknamed “blueberries,” which helped scientists investigate the history of water on Mars.\n\nEach analysis and photograph revealed new clues about that distant world.', 'Did you know the tiny spheres found by Opportunity were nicknamed blueberries because of their rounded shape? They were not fruit, but mineral formations!'],
            ['Why Was It Important?', 'Opportunity kept exploring Mars far beyond its planned lifetime.\n\nThroughout its journey, it traveled approximately 45.16 kilometers, investigating craters, rocks, and different landscapes of the Red Planet.\n\nIn June 2018, a massive dust storm covered the region where it was located. Its last communication with Earth took place on June 10 of that year.\n\nAfter several attempts to reestablish contact, NASA officially declared the mission over on February 13, 2019.\n\nOpportunity left an extraordinary legacy: it helped show that Mars once had environments with liquid water and expanded humanity’s understanding of the planet.', 'Did you know Opportunity traveled more than 45 kilometers on Mars? That was so impressive that it exceeded the length of a marathon, which is 42.195 kilometers!']],
        quiz: [['O que Opportunity procurava investigar em Mars?', ['Clues that liquid water existed in the past', 'Signs of life on Saturn', 'Places to build a station on the Moon'], 'Clues that liquid water existed in the past'], ['What happened to the Opportunity mission in 2018?', ['It returned to Earth', 'A dust storm interrupted its communications', 'It was carried away by the Spirit rover'], 'A dust storm interrupted its communications']]
    },
    ingenuity: {
        name: 'Ingenuity Mars Helicopter', dest: 'mars', place: 'Mars', type: 'Experimental helicopter · 2021–2024', summary: 'The little helicopter that conquered the skies of Mars', scenes: [
            ['A Historic Mission', 'On July 30, 2020, a mission left Earth carrying a small helicopter named Ingenuity. It was attached to the underside of the Perseverance rover, which was also on a mission to explore Mars.\n\nIngenuity was built to achieve something never done before: demonstrate that an aircraft could fly in a controlled manner on another planet.\n\nIt was small and lightweight, with a huge challenge ahead: conquering the skies of Mars!', 'Did you know Ingenuity weighed just 1.8 kg? It was so light that a person on Earth could carry it in their arms!'],
            ['Arrival on Mars', 'On February 18, 2021, Ingenuity arrived at Jezero Crater on Mars, alongside the Perseverance rover.\n\nAfter being transported to the selected location, it was time to separate from the rover and settle onto the Martian surface.\n\nBut there was a major challenge: Mars’s atmosphere is very thin, with less than 1% of the density of Earth’s atmosphere.\n\nScientists needed to find out whether its blades could generate enough lift to make it fly in that environment.', 'Did you know Ingenuity used solar power? A small panel captured sunlight to charge its batteries and enable its flights.'],
            ['What Was Left Behind?', 'On April 19, 2021, Ingenuity made its historic first flight.\n\nIts blades began spinning rapidly, and it successfully lifted off, hovered in the air, and landed again.\n\nAfter this achievement, the little helicopter continued carrying out new explorations. Its original mission called for just five demonstration flights, but it exceeded all expectations.\n\nOver the course of its journey, it completed 72 flights and traveled approximately 17 kilometers through the Martian skies.', 'Did you know Ingenuity flew as high as 24 meters? That is approximately the height of an eight-story building!'],
            ['Why Was It Important?', 'On January 18, 2024, Ingenuity made its final flight. During the attempt, its rotor blades were damaged, preventing it from flying again.\n\nNASA officially ended its mission on January 25, 2024.\n\nAlthough it could no longer explore the skies, Ingenuity made an extraordinary contribution: it demonstrated that powered, controlled flight was possible on another planet.\n\nIts experiments opened up new possibilities for future spacecraft, which may help humanity explore even more distant places.', 'Did you know Ingenuity was designed to make only five flights but managed to complete 72? Its mission lasted almost three years, far beyond the original plan!']],
        quiz: [['What was Ingenuity’s main objective?', ['To demonstrate that an aircraft could fly in a controlled manner on Mars', 'To collect rock samples using wheels', 'To transport astronauts between planets'], 'To demonstrate that an aircraft could fly in a controlled manner on Mars'], ['How many flights did Ingenuity complete during its mission?', ['5 flights', '25 flights', '72 flights'], '72 flights']]
    }
};
